00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.180
Hello everyone,

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:07.820
welcome to the Langfocus channel and my name is:

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:08.400
Plau.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.280
Today I want to look at a linguistic phenomenon that I find interesting.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.080
It's called Metathesis.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.920
The word "metathesis" comes from the Greek word "μετάθεσις",

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.600
meaning change of position, or transposition.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.280
It refers to a situation in which adjacent sounds change position in a word.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.720
For example,  in English we have the word "ask",

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.960
but you might have also heard "ax" in some dialects.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.800
You can see that the two consonants have switched places.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.360
Another example from English;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.400
we have the word "asterisk",

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.680
which refers to this little star-looking symbol.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.760
Oh wait, not that one,

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:41.480
this one.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.260
Some people pronounce it as "asteriks".

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.120
Another one.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.380
The prefix 'intro' or "intrə" (another pronunciation)

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.380
is often pronounced like "inter".

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.160
For example, "introduce", is sometimes pronounced as "interduce",

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.100
so that you can see that the "r" sound and the vowel has become reversed.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:57.780
Another one.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.080
The prefix "pre" is often pronounced like 'per'.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.720
For example, "prescription" becomes "perscription".

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.880
We can see examples of metathesis, of side by side sounds switching positions in many different languages.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.200
In what kinds of situations does this happen?

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.720
Well, during the development of a language, there's sometimes a change.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:21.920
For example, the English word "bird" came from the Old English word "bridd".

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.840
You can see that the "r" sound and the "i" became reversed.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.440
Also, the English word "thrill" came from the Middle English word "thirlen",

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.360
again, the "r" and the vowel switched positions.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.720
Second, metathesis sometimes takes places in one of two sister languages as they develop.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.520
For example, there's Spanish "preguntar"

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.240
and Portuguese "perguntar", which both mean "to ask".

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.600
And there's the Czech word "plukovník",

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.280
and the Polish word "pułkownik", which both mean "colonel", as in the military rank.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:58.760
And similarly, there's sometimes a change in certain dialects of a language but not in others, or not in the Standard language.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.640
This is like (what?) we saw in the example of "ask" and "aks".

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:09.440
"Aks" is probably the most well known example of metathesis, and it's often thought to be "broken English", or a "mistake",

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.200
but both "ask" and "aks" have been around for over a thousand years.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.320
There was Old English "ascian", as well as "acsian", which originated from metathesis.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:25.360
Eventually, "ask" became the standard pronunciation, but "aks" is still common in various dialects,

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.000
most famously in African American Vernacular English, (AAVE) also known as "Ebonics".

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:35.040
But it's also used in some other in the American South, as well as some dialects in England.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.520
You know, that city near Europe?

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:37.060
