00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:44.120
Fourth, metathesis also sometimes takes place systematically within a language, in certain phonetic or grammatical contexts.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.040
One example comes from Hebrew.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:51.560
In Hebrew most words are based on roots consisting of usually three consonants in a specific order.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:57.080
Verbs are created by placing the roots into a series of templates, that shape the meaning of the root.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.200
A typical example might be like this:

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.440
The root is k - t - v.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.720
The first template is the Pa'al form.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.560
"Katav" (כָּתַב in Hebrew).

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.120
That's the dictionary form of the verb to write.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.800
There are numerous other templates,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.160
but the key one is the "Hitpa'el template."

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.040
"Hitkatev" (הִתְכַּתֵּב in Hebrew),

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.840
meaning to correspond, as in by written letters in the old days.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.240
The hitpa'el template often gives the root reflexive or reciprocal meaning,

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.520
doing the action together with someone else.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.480
I'll get to the metathesis in just a second,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.480
this is background information for the Hebrew example.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.920
When the first consonant of a Hebrew root is an alveolar or post-alveolar fricative,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.000
like a /s/, / ʃ /, or /z/ or /ts/ sound,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.120
then the first consonant switches positions and comes before the /t/ in the hitpa'el template.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.120
For example, we have the root sh - m - sh,

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.640
which means "use".

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.720
Which in the hitpa'el template becomes

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.560
"hishtamesh" (הִשְׁתַּמֵּשׁ in Hebrew), rather than "hitshamesh" (הִתַּשְׁמֵּשׁ in Hebrew)

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.560
So you can see that the "t" and the "sh" ( ʃ ) have changed places.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.760
Metathesis can also involve the transposition of entire syllables.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.400
For example, in Japanese, we have the word "gomen", which is used to mean "I'm sorry",

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.400
but sometimes people say "mengo",

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.680
which is a humorous way of saying "sorry".

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.920
So metathesis is sometimes done intentionally for humor,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.720
but why else does it take place?

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.560
Well it's often a momentary error, sometimes, people might get confused, or nervous,

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.840
or they're thinking about two overlapping concepts at the same time, so they say the wrong thing.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.080
For example, maybe they intend to say "gold card" but they say "cold guard".

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:30.720
Also, children sometimes make errors like this when they misperceive the sounds of a word, or they have trouble reproducing them.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.640
A common example is "pasghetti" instead of "spaghetti".

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.040
But metathesis is often endu—

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:35.520
repeat? plau exposed11!111!!111 mis@3tke11!1111

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.920
But metathesis is often enduring, with shifts in words or in sound combinations,

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.040
becoming a part of that dialect or language.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.440
This could happen because certain sound combinations are slightly more awkward to articulate than others,

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.400
and people most naturally look for the path of least resistance.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.160
Also, it could be that people perceive slightly different sounds as variations of each other.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.280
For instance, vowels with different lengths or stress,

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.360
together with "r" or "l" sounds.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.000
So take the word "pretty" which is pronounced as "perty" in some dialects,

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.520
and by lots of other people trying to be funny.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.020
*roasted*

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.760
If you say "pretty" slowly,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.040
"per-ri-tty"

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:15.120
between the "p" and the "r", it sounds like there's a slight vowel sound there, like /əɹ/,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.560
so maybe people came to percive the beginning of the word /pəɹ/",

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.840
and /pɜɹ/ as close variations.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:25.400
And maybe when they say /pɜɹti/ (perty) they are perceiving a slight schwa vowel after the "r",

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.040
like /pɜɹə/, /pɜɹti/,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.720
and that slight schwa is considered a variation of the /i/ vowel.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.080
I'm speculating of course,

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.960
but if you break down the sounds, you can see where

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:38.280
people might perceive some similarity, where it's not immediately obvious to us.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.240
And there are probably other reasons for metathesis as well,

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.000
like the influence of other languages through contact.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.800
Metathesis is a very common linguistic phenomenon.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.160
If you pay attention for it, if you keep your eyes and ears open for it,

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.040
you'll probably see some examples of words that are pronounced

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.240
somewhat differently from their written form due to metathesis.

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.480
And if you enjoy examining related languages, (i do)

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.400
you'll likely notice some examples of metathesis in cognate vocabulary.

